<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calahorra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Calagurris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calahorra is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, known in Roman times as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calagurris">Calagurris</a>. It held out for Sertorius in a famine-stricken siege that ended in 72 BCE and later became a Roman municipium. It was the birthplace of the rhetorician Quintilian and probably of the Christian poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prudentius">Prudentius</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calahorra.jpg" alt="Calahorra" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zarateman · CC BY-SA 3.0 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calahorra_-_Catedral_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
